etymologies.evidence:
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:accioun|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-|id=drive}}
From {{inh|en|enm|accioun}}, {{m|enm|accion}}, from {{der|en|fro|aucion}}, {{m|fro|acciun}}, from {{der|en|la|āctiō|alt=āctiō(n)||act of doing or making}}, from {{m|la|āctus|āct(us)}} + action suffix {{m|la|-iō|-iō(n)}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|agō|agere|do, act}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂éǵeti}}. See also {{m|en|act}}, {{m|en|active}}. {{surf|+suf|en|act|ion}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*krep-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|corporacion}}, {{m|enm|corporation}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|corporatio||assumption of a body}}, from {{der|en|la|corporatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|corporō|corporare|to form into a body}}; see {{m|en|corporate}}. {{surf|en|corporate|-ion}}.

{{sense|protruding belly}} Perhaps a play on the word {{m|en|corpulence}}.

{{etymon|en|id=life science|:lbor|la-new:biologia<id:biology>|tree=1}}
{{bor+|en|la-new|biologia}} (1766), itself from {{der|en|grc|-}} {{suffix|grc|βίος|gloss1=bio-, life|λογία|gloss2=-logy, branch of study, to speak|nocat=1}}. {{surf|+af|en|bio-|-logy}}.

In English, first attested in the modern meaning in the work of English physician {{w|Thomas Beddoes}} in 1799. The term is also recorded in the sense of ''a biographical history'' in the work of {{w|Dudley Loftus}} in 1686, but this is considered by the ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' to be an isolated use. The modern {{cog|el|βιολογία}} is borrowed from the English term and {{cog|fr|biologie}} via {{w|international scientific vocabulary}}. {{piecewise doublet|en|zoology}}.

{{etymon|en|id=cicle|:inh|enm:cicle|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷel-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|cicle|t=fixed length period of years}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|cyclus}}, from {{der|en|grc|κύκλος||circle}}, from {{der|en|grk-pro||*kúklos}}, {{m|grk-pro||*kʷókʷlos}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kʷékʷlos||circle, wheel}}.

{{doublet|en|chakra|chakram|charkha|chukker|cyclus|kike|wheel}} (see there for more).

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:actyf|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-|id=drive}}
From {{inh|en|enm|actyf}}, from {{der|en|fro|actif}}, from {{der|en|la|activus|alt=āctīv(us)}}, from {{m|la|ago|agere|t=to do, to act}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂éǵeti}}. {{surf|+suf|en|act|ive}}.

{{etymon|en|id=response}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-|id=drive}}
{{inh+|en|enm|reaccion}}, from {{der|en|fro|reaction}}, from {{uder|en|la|reāctiō}}, from the verb {{m|la|reagō}}, from {{m|la|re-||again}} + {{m|la|agō||to act}}; more at {{m|en|re-}}, {{m|en|action}}; equivalent to {{suffix|en|react|ion}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂ew-|id1=perceive}}
{{ety|en|:bor|la:audītōrius|id=adjective}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|audītōrius|t=pertaining to a hearer or hearing}}, from {{af|la|audiō|-tōrius|t1=to hear|t2=-tory|pos2=adjectival suffix|nocat=1}}.

{{ety|en|:lbor|la:missiō|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mey- (change)}}
{{lbor|en|la|missiō|missiō, missiōnem|t=a sending, sending away, dispatching, discharging, release, remission, cessation}}.

[[File:Miguel de Cervantes (1605) El ingenioso hidalgo Don Quixote de la Mancha.png |thumb|200px|Front page of ''{{w|Don Quixote}}'', by {{w|Miguel de Cervantes}}, a notable example of a '''novel''']]
{{etymon|en|id=novella|:bor|it:novella|tree=1}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|novella}}, from {{der|en|la|novella}}, feminine of {{m|la|novellus}}. {{doublet|en|novella}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*mer-|id=die}}
From {{der|en|la|immortālis}}, from prefix {{m|la|im-||not}} (from {{m|la|in-}}) + {{m|la|mortālis||mortal}} (from {{m|la|mors||death}}, combining form {{m|la|mort-}} + adjectival suffix {{m|la|-alis}}), equivalent to {{af|en|im-|mortal}}. Displaced native {{m|en|undeadly}}, from {{noncog|ang|undēadlīċ}}.

The term referring to the Persian soldiers was a {{cal|en|grc|ἀθάνατος}}. One suggestion claimed that "Immortals" were named by the ancient Greeks because wounded soldiers were quickly replaced, while another claimed that it was because Greek informants confused {{der|en|peo|*anauša-||immortal, without death}} with {{der|en|peo|*anūšiya-||companion, royal bodyguard}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*mer-|id=die}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:mortal//mortel<ety:uder<fro:mortal//mortel<ety:uder<la:mortālis>>>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|mortal}}, {{m|enm|mortel}}, from {{der|en|fro|mortal}}, {{m|fro|mortel}}, and their source {{der|en|la|mortālis}}, from {{m|la|mors||death}}. In this sense, displaced native {{m|en|deadly}}, from {{noncog|ang|dēadlīċ}}.

[[File:Bicycle pedal animation.gif|thumb|Animation of a Bicycle Pedal]]
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-}}
{{ety|en|id=lever|:bor|fr:pédale<id:lever>|tree=1}}
{{bor+|en|fr|pédale}}, itself borrowed from {{der|en|it|pedale}}, from {{der|en|la|pedālis}}. {{surf|en|ped-|-al}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:reguler<ety:der<xno:reguler<ety:der<la:rēgulāris>>>>|:bor|la:rēgulāris}}
From {{inh|en|enm|reguler}}, from {{der|en|xno|reguler}}, {{der|en|frm|reguler}}, {{m|frm|regulier}}, and their source, {{der|en|la|rēgulāris|t=continuing rules for guidance}}, from {{m|la|rēgula|t=rule}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-|t=move in a straight line}}.

{{etymon|en|id=put down|:lbor|la:depositus<id:put down>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*tḱey-}}
{{lbor|en|la|depositus}}, past participle of {{m|la|depono||put down}}. {{doublet|en|depot}}.

{{ety|en|id=noun|:inh|enm:respect<ety:bor<fro:respect<ety:inh<la:respectus>>>>|tree=1}}
{{ety|en|id=verb|:denominal|respect<id:noun>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|respect}}, from {{der|en|fro|respect}}, also {{m|fro|respit||respect, regard, consideration}}, from {{der|en|la|respectus||a looking at, regard, respect}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|respiciō||look at, look back upon, respect}}, from {{m|la|re-||back}} + {{m|la|speciō||to see}}, equivalent to {{confix|en|re|spect}}. {{doublet|en|respite}}.

The [[verb]] is derived from the [[noun]].

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|dīrēctus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|dīrigō||straighten, direct}}, from {{m|la|dis-||asunder, in pieces, apart, in two}} + {{m|la|regō||make straight, rule}}. Compare [[dress]].  {{doublet|en|derecho}}.

For the meaning development compare with {{m+|ru|напра́вить||to direct, to turn, to aim, to level, to point}}, {{m|ru|отпра́вить||to send, to dispatch, to forward}} connected with {{m|ru|пра́вить||to govern, to rule, to drive, to steer}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|subséquent}}, from {{der|en|la|subsequentis}}, form of {{m|la|subsequēns}}, present participle of
{{m|la|subsequor||to follow, to succeed}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*wekʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|advocat}}, {{m|enm|advoket}}, from {{der|en|fro|advocat}}, from {{der|en|la|advocātus||an advocate}}, from the [[substantivization]] of the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|advoco|advocāre|to call for}} (see {{af|en|-ate|id1=substantive|pos1=noun-forming suffix}}), from a [[calque]] of {{noncog|grc|παράκλητος}} (whence {{cog|en|paraclete}}). 

{{doublet|en|advoke|avocat|avouch|avow}}. The [[verb]] derives from the [[noun]] on the basis of {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyd-}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:visioun|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|visioun}}, from {{der|en|xno|visioun}}, from {{der|en|fro|vision}}, from {{der|en|la|vīsiō|t=vision, seeing}}, noun of action from the perfect passive participle {{m|la|visus|t=that which is seen}}, from the verb {{m|la|videō|t=to see}} + action noun suffix {{m|la|-iō}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷem-}}
{{etymon|en|:bor|frm:convention<ety:bor<la:conventiō<alt:conventiōnem>>>}}
Recorded since about 1440, borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|convention}}, from {{der|en|la|conventiō||meeting, assembling; agreement, convention}}, from {{m|la|conveniō||come, gather or meet together, assemble}}, from {{m|la|con-||with, together}} + {{m|la|veniō||come}}. Equivalent to {{suf|en|convene|tion}}.

[[File:Häststall Elfviks gård dec 2008.jpg|thumb|The [[interior]] of a [[horse]] stable ''(sense 1)'']]
{{root|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
{{etymon|en|id=building for horses|:inh|enm:stable<id:building for horses>|tree=1|text=3}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stable}}, borrowed from {{der|en|xno|estable|stable}}, from {{der|en|la|stabulum|stab(u)lum}}.

[[File:017-Scale.jpg|thumb|Stable ''(sense 1)'' [[scales]]]]
{{etymon|en|id=unchanging|:inh|enm:stable<id:unchanging>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stable}}, from {{der|en|xno|stable}}, {{m|fro|stabel}}, from {{der|en|la|stabilis||firm, steadfast}} (itself from {{m|la|stare||stand}} + {{m|la|-abilis||able}}). Displaced native {{ncog|ang|staþolfæst}}.

{{etymon|en|:bor|la:cōnstrūctus}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*strew-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|cōnstrūctus}}, from {{m|la|cōnstruō||to heap together}}, from {{m|la|com-||together}} + {{m|la|struō||to heap up, pile}}. {{doublet|en|construe}}.

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:voluntarie}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|voluntarie}}, from {{der|en|fro|volontaire}}, from {{der|en|la|voluntārius||willing, of free will}}, from {{m|la|voluntās||will, choice, desire}}, from {{m|la|volēns}}, present participle of {{m|la|volo||to will}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|selfwille|lit=self-willed}}.

{{etymon|en|:der|fr:volition|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-}}
From {{der|en|fr|volition}}, from {{der|en|la-med|volitiō||will, volition}}, from {{der|en|la|volō||to wish; to want; to mean or intend}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*welh₁-||to choose; to want}}) + {{m|la|-tiō|pos=suffix forming nouns relating to some action or the result of an action}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*-tis|pos=suffix forming abstract or action nouns from verbs}}).

{{etymon|en|:bor|la-new:automaticus<ety:from<la-cla:automatum>>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*men-|id=think}}
{{bor+|en|la-new|automaticus}}, from {{der|en|la-cla|automatum|t=[[automaton]]}} + {{m|la|-icus|pos=adjectival suffix}}, from {{der|en|grc|αὐτόματον}}, neuter of {{m|grc|αὐτόματος||self-moving, moving of oneself, self-acting, spontaneous}}, from {{m|grc|αὐτός||self, myself}} + {{m|grc|μέμαα||to wish eagerly, strive, yearn, desire}}. The original pronunciation, apparently with stress on the second syllable, was after the ultimate Greek base.

{{rfe|en|OED says this comes from middle/old French, then from Latin}}
{{etymon|en|:der|la:adiacēns|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(H)yeh₁-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|adiacēns}}, {{m|la|adiacentis}}, derivative of {{m|la|adiaceō||to lie beside}}; from {{m|la|ad||to}} + {{m|la|iaceō||to lie down}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*speḱ-}}
{{etymon|en|id=optical instrument|:bor|it:microscopio<id:optical instrument>|tree=1}}
From {{bor|en|la-new|microscopium}} and {{bor|en|it|microscopio}}, from {{der|en|grc|μικρός||small}} + {{m|grc|σκοπέω||to look at}}, equivalent to {{af|en|micro-|-scope}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeydʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|confidence}}, from {{der|en|la|cōnfīdentia}} (possibly via {{der|en|fro|confidence}}), from {{m|la|cōnfīdō||believe, confide in}} from {{m|la|con-||with}} + {{m|la|fīdō||trust}}.

{{surf|en|confide|ence|nocat=1}}.

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:fredom<alt:freedom>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*preyH-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fredom}}, {{m|enm|freedom}}, from {{inh|en|ang|frēodōm|t=freedom, state of free-will, charter, emancipation, deliverance}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*frijadōm|t=freedom}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|free|dom}}. Cognate with {{cog|frr|fridoem|t=freedom}}, {{cog|nl|vrijdom|t=freedom}}, {{cog|nds|frīdom|t=freedom}}, {{cog|gmh|vrītuom|t=freedom}}, {{cog|no|fridom|t=freedom}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*sed-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|resident}}, from {{der|en|xno|resident}}, from {{der|en|la|residēns}}, present participle of {{m|la|resideō||to remain behind, reside, dwell}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|reside|ent}}, from {{m|la|re-||back}} + {{m|la|sedeō||to sit}}. {{doublet|en|resiant|rezident}}. The espionage sense is a {{semantic loan|en|ru|резиде́нт|nocap=1}}.

{{sense|physician}} So called because in the 19th century they resided in the hospital dormitories.

From {{af|en|bake|-ery<t:place of>}}. Replaced earlier {{m|en|bakehouse}}. Originally "place for making bread"; as "shop where baked goods are sold", it was believed by British travelers by 1832 to be an [[American]]-invented term.

{{etystub|en|Were Swedish/Danish {{m|sv|bageri}} calqued from English or vice versa? What is the history?}}

Sense 4 is by analogy with {{m|en|cake||buttocks}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyḱ-|*nem-|id2=distribute}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:economike<alt:economike, iconomique><ety:bor<enm:iconomique<ety:der<fro:iconomike<ety:der<la:oeconomicus>>>>>>|tree=1}}

The {{lg|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|economike}}, {{m|enm|iconomique|t=household management}},{{ref|{{R:MED|īconomique|n|MED21657}}}} and then:

* from {{der|en|frm|iconomique}}, {{m|frm|oeconomique}}, and {{der|en|fro|iconomike|t=(''noun'') household management; person in charge of household management; (''adjective'') relating to household management; relating to domestic or family matters; relating to management of a state; reducing costs or expenses, economical}} (modern {{cog|fr|économique}}); and
* from their {{lg|etymon}} {{der|en|la|oeconomicus|t=(''noun'') household manager, housekeeper, steward; (''adjective'') relating to orderly arrangement of written material}} (whence {{cog|la-lat|economicus|t=relating to (management of) a household}}), and {{m|la|economica|t=household management}}, both from {{der|en|grc|οἰκονομῐκός|t=skilled in household management; frugal, thrifty, economical}} (whence {{cog|grc-koi|οἰκονομῐκός|t=relating to orderly arrangement of written material}});

from {{m|grc|οἰκονόμος|t=master of a house; household manager, steward; administrator, manager}} + {{m|grc|-ῐκός|pos={{lg|suffix}} meaning ‘of or relating to’ forming {{lg|adjective}}s}}.{{ref|name=OED|{{R:OED|economic|n.'' & ''adj|7782161419|March 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}}} {{m|grc||Οἰκονόμος}} is derived from {{m|grc|οἶκος|t=dwelling place, house; estate}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*weyḱ-|t=(''verb'') to enter in; to settle; (''noun'') settlement}}) + {{m|grc|νόμος|t=law, ordinance}} (from {{m|grc|νέμω|t=to distribute; to possess}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*nem-|t=to distribute; to give; to take}}) + {{m|grc|-ος|pos=suffix forming [[o-grade]] action nouns from verbs}}).

{{senseno|en|Christianity|uc=1}} (“one who manages the income of a vacant benefice”) is from {{der|en|it|economo}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|oeconomus|t=housekeeper, steward; administrator, especially of a religious foundation}}, from {{der|en|grc|οἰκονόμος|t=master of a house; household manager, steward; administrator, manager}}: see above.{{ref|name=OED}}

The {{lg|adjective}} is derived from the noun; compare the adjective senses in Middle and Old French, Latin, and Ancient Greek.{{ref|name=OED}}

{{surf|en|economy|-ic}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂yew-|*dʰeh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|justifien}}, from {{der|en|fro|justifier}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|justificare||make just}}, from {{der|en|la|justus}}, {{m|la|iustus||just}} + {{m|la|ficare||make}}, from {{m|la|facere}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|just|ify}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:fluid<ety:der<la:fluidus>>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰel- (blow)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fluid}}, from {{der|en|la|fluidus|t=flowing; fluid}}, from {{der|en|la|fluō|t=to flow}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰleh₁-|t=to swell; surge; overflow; run}}. Akin to {{cog|grc|φλύειν|t=to swell; overflow}}. Not related to {{noncog|en|flow}}, which is a native, inherited word from {{m|ine-pro|*plew-}}, but is distantly related from {{cog|en|bleat}}.

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:doctour|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deḱ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|doctor}}, {{m|enm|doctour||an expert, authority on a subject}}, from {{der|en|xno|doctour}}, from {{der|en|la|doctor||teacher}}, from {{m|la|doceō||to teach}}. Displaced native {{cog|enm|lerare||doctor, teacher}} (from {{cog|enm|leren||to teach, instruct}} from {{cog|ang|læran|lǣran, lēran|to teach, instruct, guide}}, compare {{cog|ang|lārēow||teacher, master}}). Displaced {{cog|ang|lǣċe||doctor, physician}}.

{{etymon|en|id=part|:inh|enm:fraccioun<id:fracture>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fraccioun||a breaking}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}}, {{der|en|fro|fraccion}}, from {{der|en|la-med|fractio||a fragment, portion}}, from earlier {{der|en|la|fractio||a breaking, a breaking into pieces}}, from {{m|la|fractus}}, past participle of {{m|la|frangere||to break}} (whence English {{m|en|frangible}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}} (English {{m|en|break}}). {{dbt|en|frazione}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}

Probably from {{af|en|factor|-y}}, although in {{senseno|en|Q83405|t=a place where manufacturing takes place}} apparently influenced strongly by association with {{der|en|la-cla||fact-}}, the {{glossary|past participle|past participial}} {{glossary|stem}} of {{m|la|faciō|t=to make}}; compare {{m|en|manufactory}} and {{cog|la|factōrium|t=an oil press}}. In {{senseno|en|trading establishment|t=a trading establishment}}, originally after {{der|en|pt|feitoria}}. In {{senseno|en|invoice|t=an invoice or inventory}}, probably after obsolete {{der|en|nl|facture|t=bill}}; see {{m|en|facture}}.
Compare {{cog|nl|factorij}}, {{cog|frm|factorie}}, {{cog|es|factoría}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:reducen<ety:der<fro:reduire>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|reducen}}, from {{der|en|fro|reduire}}, from {{der|en|la|redūcō||reduce}}; from {{m|la|re-||back}} + {{m|la|dūcō||lead}}. See {{m|en|duke}}, and compare with {{m|en|redoubt}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:fragment}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm|fragment}}, from {{der|en|la|fragmentum|t=a fragment, remnant}}, from {{suf|la|frangō|t1=to break|mentum|nocat=1}}.

{{etymon|en|:der|la:trānsferō|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{der|en|la|trānsferō|t=to bear across}}. Distant {{dbt|en|translate|nocap=1}}. See also [[collate]] and [[confer]], [[delate]] and [[defer]], as well as [[prelate]] and [[prefer]] among others.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂welh₁-|id=rule}}
From {{uder|en|la|aequivalentem}}, accusative singular of {{m|la|aequivalēns}}, present active participle of {{m|la|aequivaleō||to be equivalent, have equal power}}. {{surf|+con|en|equi|valent}}.

Mostly displaced native {{ncog|enm|efenmete}}.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*deyḱ-}}
{{ety|en|:bor|la:dictātum|id=an order, a command|tree=1}}
{{etydate|1581}}; {{bor+|en|la|dictātum|t=a thing said, something dictated|nocap=1}}, [[substantivize]]d from the {{glossary|nominative}} {{glossary|neuter}} {{glossary|singular}} of {{m|la|dictātus}}, the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|dictō||pronounce or declare repeatedly; dictate}}, {{glossary|frequentative}} of {{m|la|dīcō||say, speak}}. {{doublet|en|diktat}}.

