Cognate with {{cog|sco|-in}}, {{m|sco|-in'}}, {{m|sco|-ing|t=-ing}}, {{cog|yol|-een|t=-ing}}, {{cog|stq|-enge|t=-ing}}, {{cog|nl|-ing|t=-ing}}, {{cog|de,lb|-ung|t=-ing}}, {{cog|da|-ing}}, {{m|da|-ning|t=-ing}}, {{cog|is,nb,nn,sv|-ing|t=-ing}}, {{cog|fr|-ange|t=-ing}}.

{{glossary|false cognate|Unrelated}} to {{noncog|brh|-اِنْگ|tr=-iṅg|t=a suffix used to form infinitive or verbal noun from the base verb; -ing}}.
{{col-bottom}}

{{etymon|en|id=participial|:inh|enm:-ynge<id:present participle>}}
From {{der|en|enm|-inge}}, {{m|enm|-ynge}}, alteration of earlier {{m|enm|-inde}}, {{m|enm|-ende}}, {{m|enm|-and}} (see {{l|en|-and}}), from {{inh|en|ang|-ende|pos=present participle ending}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*-andī}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*-andz|pos=present participle ending}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*-onts}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fy|-end}}, {{cog|nl|-end}}, {{cog|af|-ende}}, {{cog|de|-end}}, {{cog|nds|-end}}, {{cog|da|-ende}}, {{cog|sv|-ande}}, {{cog|is|-andi}}, {{cog|got|-𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃}}, {{m|got|-𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃}}, {{cog|la|-ans}}, {{m|la|-ans|-ant-}}, {{cog|grc|-ων}}, {{cog|sa|-अन्त्}}, {{cog|fa|ـنده|tr=-ande}}, {{cog|pl|-ący}}, {{m|pl|-ąc}}, {{cog|cs|-oucí}}, {{cog|uk|-учий}}, {{cog|sh|-ući}}/{{m|sh|-ући}}. More at {{m|en|-and}}.

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:dis-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dis-}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|dis-}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dwís}}. {{pwd|en|bis-}}; further related to {{dbt|en|bi-|di-|twi-|notext=1|nocat=1}}.

Often confused with the separately inherited prefix {{m|en|dys-}}.

{{ety|la|title=dis-|id=apart|:inh|itc-pro:*dis-<ety:inh<ine-pro:*dwís<alt:*d(w)is-><t:in two, apart>>>|tree=+}}
{{inh+|la|itc-pro|*dis-}}, with the exception of the uncommon {{cog|got|𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃-}}, interestingly largely displaced by the likely Latin-influenced {{cog|got|𐌳𐌹𐍃-}}. It is not evident whether the {{m|ine-pro||*w}}-less form associated with the meaning {{m-g|apart}} is to be dated as late as Italic and Germanic or back to Proto-Indo-European. Some have attributed the loss of {{m|ine-pro||*w}} to dissimilation when prefixed to verbs starting in {{m|ine-pro||*w-}}, e.g. {{m|la|dīvidō}}, {{m|la|dīvertō}}, then regularised to the other cases.

The meaning of the prefix in the formation {{m|la|difficilis|t=difficult}} from {{m|la|facilis|t=easy}} cannot be immediately parsed as {{m-g|apart}}. It has been explained as built on the example of {{m|la|similis}} : {{m|la|dissimilis}}, which in turn arose as the opposition of {{m|la|consimilis}}, in the example of common pairs like {{m|la|distrahō}} : {{m|la|contrahō}}, etc. Alternatively it has also been analysed as an initially independent but later conflated prefixation with {{cog|ine-pro|*dus-|t=bad}},

{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:ex-<ety:bor<frm:ex-<ety:der<la:ex->>>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|-}}, from words borrowed from {{der|en|frm|-}}; from {{der|en|la|ex||out of, from}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*eǵ-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*eǵs-||out}}, {{m|ine-pro|*eǵʰs}}. Cognate with {{cog|grc|ἐξ||out of, from}}, {{cog|xtg|ex-||out}}, {{cog|sga|ess-||out}}, {{cog|cu|изъ||out}}, {{cog|ru|из||from, out of}}.

For {{senseno|en|former|t=former}}, compare sense 11 at {{m+|la|ex|pos=indicates a change of state, (later) used before terms denoting an office to indicate that one has completed his term of office}}.<!--It might have been rebracketed as a prefix--> It is probably a semantic extension from the sense of "out, away".

From an alteration of earlier {{m|en|neighborred||neighborhood}}, from {{der|en|enm|neȝeburredde}}, {{m|enm|neheborreden}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|neighbor|red|nocat=1}}; the term being interpreted as {{af|en|neighbor|-hood}}. For change in suffix ({{m|en|-red}} to {{m|en|-hood}}), compare {{m|en|brotherhood}}.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|nichbourheid|t=neighbourhood}}. Compare also {{cog|nl|naburigheid|t=neighbourhood}}.
{{col-bottom}}

{{etymon|la|id=forward|:inh|itc-pro:*pro-<id:forward>|pos=prefix}}
{{root|la|ine-pro|*per-|id=before}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*pro-}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*pro-}}. Synchronically served as the prefix counterpart to the preposition {{m|la|prō}}.

{{etymon|la|id=end}}
{{unk|la|Disputed}}. Perhaps for {{m+|la||*fignis}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*dʰeygʷ-|t=to stick, set up}}, whence {{m|la|fīgō}}, or for {{m+|la||*fidnis}}, from {{m+|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-|t=to split}}, whence {{m|la|findō}}. {{rfv-etym|la}}
<br/>Other hypotheses include:
* From {{m+|ine-pro|*bʰeyh₂-|*bʰeyH-|to strike|id=strike}}; compare {{m|la|*perfinō|perfinēs|(you would) break, shatter}}.
* From the same source as {{cog|gem-pro|*bainaz||straight; ready}} and {{m|gem-pro|*bainą||bone}}, from {{m+|ine-pro||*bʰeyH-n-|something straight, pole}}, which may or may not be from the same root “to strike” above.

For the meaning “region”, compare {{m|la|pāgus}} again from a root meaning “to fix”.

{{etymon|la|title=faciō|id=to do|:inh|itc-pro:*fakjō<id:to make>|tree=1}}
{{inh+|la|itc-pro|*fakjō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-|*dʰh₁k-yé-ti|to put, place}}. {{doublet|la|-ficō}}.

The passive voice is [[suppletive]]ly provided by the etymologically unrelated {{m|la|fīō||to become}}.

{{root|la|ine-pro|*bʰlewH-}}
{{etymon|la|title=fluō|:inh|ine-pro:*bʰluH-yé-ti<unc><ety:der<ine-pro:*bʰlewH-<id:overflow>>>|id=flow|tree=1}}
Ultimately from {{der|la|ine-pro|*bʰlewH-||to overflow}}, possibly an extension of {{m|ine-pro|*bʰleh₁-||to swell, blow}}. The Latin form may have developed from earlier ''*flowō'' via vowel reduction (which was regular only in non-initial syllables, but may have been introduced to the simple verb by analogy with its compounds)  from [[Proto-Indo-European]] ''*bʰleuH-(e/o)''. Alternatively, it may go back to {{inh|la|itc-pro|*flūō}}, from earlier ''*flūjō'', from [[Proto-Indo-European]] ''*bʰluH-yé-ti''. Cognate with {{cog|grc|φλέω||to abound}}, {{m|grc|φλύω||to boil over}}. Unrelated to {{noncog|en|flow}}, despite phonological and semantic similarity.

{{etymon|la|:from|itc-ala:kapiō<id:take>|id=take}}
From {{inh|la|itc-ala|kapiō}}, from {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kapjō}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*kap-||to seize, grab}}. More specifically, according to the LIV, it reflects {{inh|la|ine-pro|*kh₂pyéti|*kapyéti}}, whence also {{cog|gem-pro|*habjaną|t=to lift, heave}}. However, Schrijver instead suggests that the term reflects {{inh|la|itc-pro||*kapiti ~ *kapienti}}, itself—according to De Vaan—from an athematic i-present {{m|ine-pro||*kh₂p-i-}} or {{m|ine-pro||*kap-i-}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sq|kap}}, {{cog|br|kavout}}, {{cog|cy|cael}}, {{cog|en|have}}, {{m|en|heave}}, {{cog|lv|kàmpt}}{{,}} and {{cog|grc|κάπτω}}.

{{root|la|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
{{ety|la|tree=1|:inh<unc>|itc-pro:*staðlis<ety:af<*staēō<id:to stand>><*-ðlis<id:est>>>|:af<surf>|stō<id:to stand>|-bilis<id:est>}}
{{surf|la|stō<id:to stand><t: to stand (still), remain standing, be upright, be erect>|-bilis}}. Poultney compares the term to {{cog|xum|𐌔𐌕𐌀𐌚𐌋𐌉}} and reconstructs {{inh|la|itc-pro|*staðlis}},{{ref|{{R:xum:Poultney:1959|324}}}} though De Vaan leaves the etymology of the Umbrian term as uncertain.{{ref|{{R:itc:EDL|pages=389{{ndash}}390|head=stō, stāre|passage=Derivatives > line 21 > ''stabilis''}}}}

{{etymon|la|id=water|:inh|itc-pro:*akʷā<ety:inh<ine-pro:*h₂ékʷeh₂<id:water>>>}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro||*akʷā}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*h₂ékʷeh₂}}. Cognate with {{cog|gem-pro|*ahwō|t=water, stream}}.

The few cognates in other Indo-European branches point to a narrower original meaning of "running water". See {{ncog|xum|𐌖𐌕𐌖𐌓|t=water}}, which instead represents a remnant Italic continuation of {{ncog|ine-pro|*wódr̥}}, the more widespread word for water in Indo-European languages.

