{{root|en|ine-pro|*stel-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|local}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|locālis|t=belonging to a place}}, possibly also via {{der|en|fro|local}}; ultimately from {{der|en|la|locus|t=a place}}.

The {{l|en|local|id=ring|ring-theoretic senses}} derive from {{w|Wolfgang Krull|Krull}}, who first referred to Noetherian commutative rings with a unique maximal ideal as "Stellenring" ({{compound|de|Stellen|ring|t1=place|nocat=1}}) in 1938. The term was inspired by [[algebraic geometry]], where local rings encode information about the behavior of curves (surfaces, etc.) at points; hence, describe "local" behavior.

{{etymon|en|:uder|frm:géographie<ety:uder<la:geōgraphia>>|tree=1}}
From {{uder|en|frm|géographie}}, from {{uder|en|la|geōgraphia}}, from {{uder|en|grc|γεωγραφία||a [[description]] of the [[earth]]}}, from {{m|grc|γῆ||[[earth]]}} + {{m|grc|γράφω||[[write]]}}. {{doublet|en|geographia}}.

Use in reference to lavatories derives from the mid-20th century euphemism "show one the geography of the house" in reference to pointing out the toilets.

{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂yew-}}
